{"hash":"ed0fb3657ef73d32","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":88.811,"said":"This small shrine holds the linga Hanuman brought. Picture his arrival. He lands with the linga from Kailash in his hands, and finds the worship already done, Sita’s linga of sand already installed. He is heartbroken. All that flight, for nothing? A well-loved telling says he even wrapped his tail around the sand linga to pull it up and set his own in its place, and could not move it at all. Rama understood his friend’s sorrow. He installed Hanuman’s linga here, beside his own, and gave a command: whoever comes to worship at Rameshwaram must first worship this linga. And so it has been ever since. Pilgrims come here first, and only then go on to Ramanathaswamy. Now listen to its name. Hanuman’s linga is the Vishwalingam, and this shrine is Kasi Viswanathar, the Lord of Kashi, the very name of the Lord who lives far away on the Ganga. Remember the sand from the beach? Here the two holy places meet, Kashi in the north and Rameshwaram in the south, bound into one pilgrimage. You have done as Rama asked. Now turn to the inner door. Ramanathaswamy is waiting.\n\nDid you know? Rameshwaram is also one of the four dhams of India, with Badrinath, Puri and Dwarka, and the only one of the twelve Jyotirlingas in Tamil Nadu.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1070},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1086,"end":1229}],"words":[[0.098,0.24,0,4],[0.35,0.361,5,10],[0.723,0.421,11,17],[1.156,0.325,18,23],[1.492,0.108,24,27],[1.613,0.337,28,33],[1.961,0.529,34,41],[2.502,0.469,42,49],[3.87,0.361,51,58],[4.243,0.204,59,62],[4.459,0.625,63,70],[5.949,0.132,72,74],[6.094,0.373,75,80],[6.478,0.132,81,85],[6.623,0.072,86,89],[6.707,0.361,90,95],[7.079,0.24,96,100],[7.332,0.481,101,108],[7.824,0.108,109,111],[7.945,0.168,112,115],[8.125,0.457,116,121],[8.846,0.252,123,126],[9.111,0.397,127,132],[9.519,0.12,133,136],[9.651,0.493,137,144],[10.156,0.481,145,152],[10.649,0.373,153,157],[11.262,0